I think that you'd be best off asking about both DFS and AFS.  The
reason being that one was evolved from the other and there are quite a
few examples of production AFS usage in the both the academic/research
and business world.   In addition, anyone using AFS is very likely 
contemplating migrating to DFS in the near future.
  
In the AFS case, there are quite a few "real" examples of AFS in use
at scales, that these other filesystems only dream about.  There are
many examples of AFS cells with user populations of 1 - 10K or higher.
There are also cells with aggregate storage measured in TeraBytes.  In
addition, there are example of Corporations with mutliple cells.
(Last time I counted, IBM had over 50 active cells) That's real
Enterprise scalability, not vapor.

I have never seen this duplicated in any of these other environments.
Add the some of the other features like security and replication and
the story even gets better.  

Hope this helps,


 
